#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""ci-local: run the repository's gates on this Mac, after showing that each one still tells good input from bad.

Usage:
    tools/ci-local [--base <ref>] [--body <file>] [--controls-only] [--commit <rev>]

With --commit the run judges that commit, not the working tree: it is checked out in a throwaway clone that shares
this repository's objects, and the manifest, controls and gates are read from it, so what is judged is exactly what a
push sends. Nothing is written to this repository.

Steps, in order, and the run stops at the first step that does not hold:

1. Coverage. Every gate that .github/workflows/gates.yml calls, by path or through its `for pair in` list, needs an
   entry in tools/ci-local.toml; every entry needs an executable in tools/gates and, under tools/ci-controls/<gate>,
   a folder named good and one or more named bad or bad-<label>. Each bad folder holds expect.txt, the message
   that gate gives for that particular input, so a gate that fails some other way is not counted as refusing. A
   gate that reads a pull request body also needs pr-body.md in each folder.
   The workflow text is read whole: wherever it says tools/gates, that spot has to be a gate path, a table file
   (.toml, .txt or .json), or "tools/gates/$name" inside a `for pair in` loop this reader understood. A spot that is
   none of these stops the run, because a gate called that way would get no controls. Every other tracked workflow
   under .github/workflows is read the same way, and each gate it names, comments included (a # inside a quoted
   string is no comment), must be a manifest gate or an [others] entry whose runs_in is that workflow. Every
   executable in tools/gates is a manifest gate or an [others] entry, and an [others] entry either names the
   workflow that runs it, which must name the gate on a line outside a comment, or says not_run with a reason, so no
   gate is left run by nothing. ci-local does not read local actions, so a tracked one (anything under
   .github/actions, or any action.yml or action.yaml) stops the run until it does.
2. Controls. Each folder becomes a throwaway repository: an empty first commit, then the folder's files in a second
   commit whose message is commit-message.txt if present (that file, pr-body.md and expect.txt are not committed). A
   file named
   <name>.template is written as <name> with {{}} removed and each {{U+XXXX}} turned into that character, so the
   stored copy of a bad case never matches what the gates look for in this repository. The gate runs
   on it with the range first..second and, for a body gate, PR_BODY set from pr-body.md. good must exit 0; each bad
   folder must exit 1 and print its expect.txt text, since a crash exits 1 too. Output holding a Python traceback is a
   crash in either case. Anything else (another status, no exit within the entry's timeout, more than 4 MiB on either
   stream) prints why on stderr, and the run ends with status 2 before any gate looks at this repository.
3. This repository. Each gate runs once, with the range <base>..HEAD (default base origin/main; --base none means
   no commit is published yet, so the range is HEAD and all of its history), and prints PASS, FAIL or BROKEN. A body
   gate is NOT RUN unless --body names the text to use, and a gate that takes the range is NOT RUN when the range
   holds no commit. The last line counts them: "summary: P passed, F failed, B broken, N not run".

A gate starts a new session, and when it returns, times out, overflows its output or ci-local is stopped, every
process still in that session's group gets SIGTERM and then SIGKILL. That reaches only the group: a child that calls
setsid on its own walks out of it. Gates are code from the commit under test and run as you, so this tool limits
what they leave lying around by accident; it does not contain them.

Every gate gets a reduced environment: a PATH of one fresh folder, holding links to the caller's own python3, git
and Rust tools as the caller's PATH finds them, then the system folders; its own fresh HOME, CARGO_HOME and TMPDIR
for each invocation; RUSTUP_HOME for the installed toolchains; no global or system git config; and PR_BODY for body
gates. Nothing else the caller has set reaches it, so a Cargo config on this Mac changes no result. One thing does:
a run on the working tree is a run inside this repository, so its own .git/config applies to the gates' git commands
as it does to any git command here; --commit judges a fresh clone, which has none of it. Stopping a gate's group and
reaping it run with SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP held, only the first stop counts, a stop that arrives while a gate is
being started waits until its cleanup is in place, no group is signalled once its leader is gone, and a gate that
cannot be collected makes the run broken.

Manifest entries:
    [gates.<name>]
    args = ["{repo}", "{range}"]   # {repo} is the repository path, {range} the commit range
    body = true                    # optional: the gate reads PR_BODY
    timeout = 600                  # optional: seconds per run
    copy = ["LICENSE", "crates/"]  # optional: tracked files (a folder: all under it) put in both controls first
    fixture = "rust-workspace"     # optional: a fixed tree under tools/ci-controls/fixtures laid down first, so a
                                   # control never depends on the product it is about to judge; its files, like a
                                   # control's, may end in .template to stay out of the gates' view of this repo

Locally a gate reads the tables in this tree; the gates workflow alone judges with the base commit's tables.

Exit 0: every gate that ran passed; the NOT RUN lines and the summary say what did not run. 1: a gate failed on this
repository. 2: broken or refused.
"""
import contextlib
import os
import pathlib
import re
import selectors
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import tomllib

WORKFLOW = pathlib.Path(".github/workflows/gates.yml")
WORKFLOWS = pathlib.Path(".github/workflows")
WORKFLOW_SUFFIXES = (".yml", ".yaml")
MAX_WORKFLOWS = 64
OTHER_KINDS = ("runs_in", "not_run")
MANIFEST = pathlib.Path("tools/ci-local.toml")
CONTROLS = pathlib.Path("tools/ci-controls")
FIXTURES = CONTROLS / "fixtures"
GATES = pathlib.Path("tools/gates")
KEYS = {"args", "body", "timeout", "copy", "fixture"}
PLACEHOLDERS = {"{repo}", "{range}"}
DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 600
NAME = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*")
CALLED = re.compile(r"tools/gates/([a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*)(?![A-Za-z0-9._-])")
LOOP = re.compile(r"\bfor\s+pair\s+in\s+([^;\n]*);")
MESSAGE = "commit-message.txt"
TEMPLATE = ".template"
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"\{\{(.*?)\}\}")
CODE_POINT = re.compile(r"U\+([0-9A-F]{4,6})")
BODY = "pr-body.md"
EXPECT = "expect.txt"
BAD_FOLDER = re.compile(r"bad(-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*)?")
OUTPUT_CAP = 4 * 1024 * 1024
TRACEBACK = b"Traceback (most recent call last)"
MENTION = re.compile(r"tools/gates")
PIECE = re.compile(r"gates/")
TABLE_MENTION = re.compile(r"tools/gates/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*\.(?:toml|txt|json)(?![A-Za-z0-9._-])")
LOOP_VARIABLE = re.compile(r"tools/gates/\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?![A-Za-z0-9_{])")
DONE = re.compile(r"\bdone\b")
MAX_MANIFEST = 256 * 1024
MAX_WORKFLOW = 1024 * 1024
MAX_TRACKED = 20000
MAX_TRACKED_BYTES = 16 * 1024 * 1024
MAX_BODY = 256 * 1024
GIT_OUTPUT_CAP = 1024 * 1024
CHUNK = 64 * 1024
MAX_CONTROL_FILES = 500
MAX_CONTROL_BYTES = 8 * 1024 * 1024
IDENTITY = {"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "ci-local", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "ci-local@example.invalid",
            "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "ci-local", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "ci-local@example.invalid"}


class Broken(Exception):
    pass


def say(text):
    print(text, flush=True)


def complain(text):
    print(text, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


def git(args, cwd, env, timeout=120, cap=GIT_OUTPUT_CAP):
    status, output, errors, why = run_bounded(["git", *args], cwd, env, timeout, cap)
    if why:
        raise Broken(f"git {' '.join(args[:2])} {why}")
    if status != 0:
        raise Broken(f"git {' '.join(args[:2])} failed: {errors.decode('utf-8', 'replace').strip()[:300]}")
    return output.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")


def run_bounded(argv, cwd, env, timeout, cap):
    """(status, stdout, stderr, why) for argv, run with no input in a session of its own. Both pipes are read as data
    arrives and counted; once either passes cap bytes, or the deadline passes, the process group is stopped and status
    is None, with why saying which. When the process itself exits its group is stopped too, and the pipes are read to
    their end, so a child it left in the group cannot hold the run open; one that moved to a session of its own and
    keeps a pipe open runs the clock out instead. Pipes that end first are not the end: a process that closed its
    output is waited for until the deadline. Nothing goes to a file and nothing is read whole: every subprocess
    ci-local reads from runs here."""
    keep_child_status()
    held = signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, STOPS)  # no stop between the start and the cleanup block below
    try:
        process = subprocess.Popen([str(part) for part in argv], cwd=str(cwd), env=env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                   stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, start_new_session=True)
    except BaseException:
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_SETMASK, held)
        raise
    out_fd, err_fd = process.stdout.fileno(), process.stderr.fileno()
    taken = {out_fd: bytearray(), err_fd: bytearray()}
    why = None
    stopped = False
    collected = False
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    try:
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_SETMASK, held)  # first thing inside: a stop now lands where cleanup runs
        with selectors.DefaultSelector() as watch:
            for stream in (process.stdout, process.stderr):
                watch.register(stream.fileno(), selectors.EVENT_READ)
            while watch.get_map() and why is None:
                left = deadline - time.monotonic()
                if left <= 0:
                    why = f"did not finish within {timeout} seconds"
                    break
                if not stopped and leader_exited(process):
                    stop_group(process)  # what it left in its group would hold the pipes; the leader is not reaped yet
                    stopped = True
                for key, _events in watch.select(timeout=min(left, 0.25)):
                    data = os.read(key.fd, CHUNK)
                    if not data:
                        watch.unregister(key.fd)
                        continue
                    taken[key.fd] += data
                    if len(taken[key.fd]) > cap:
                        why = f"wrote more than {cap // (1024 * 1024)} MiB of output"
                        break
        if why is None and not wait_exit(process, deadline - time.monotonic()):  # pipes ended; it may still work
            why = f"did not finish within {timeout} seconds"
    finally:
        with stops_held():  # a stop arriving now waits until the gate's group is gone and its leader reaped
            if not stopped:
                stop_group(process)
            collected = reap(process)  # only now: every signal above went to a group whose leader still held its id
            process.stdout.close()
            process.stderr.close()
    if not collected:
        raise Broken(f"{os.path.basename(str(argv[0]))} could not be collected after its group was stopped")
    if process.returncode is None or not leader_was_held(process):
        raise Broken(f"{os.path.basename(str(argv[0]))}'s exit status was lost (is SIGCHLD ignored?)")
    return (None if why else process.returncode), bytes(taken[out_fd][:cap]), bytes(taken[err_fd][:cap]), why


def called_gates(text):
    """The gate names the workflow runs: each tools/gates/<name> path and each name in a `for pair in` list."""
    names = set(CALLED.findall(text))
    for listing in LOOP.findall(text):
        for pair in listing.split():
            name = pair.split(":", 1)[0]
            if not NAME.fullmatch(name):
                raise Broken(f"cannot read the gate name {name!r} in the workflow's `for pair in` list")
            names.add(name)
    return names


def read_capped(path, cap, what):
    """The file's text, refused when it holds more than cap bytes, so nothing a commit carries is read unbounded."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK | os.O_NOFOLLOW)  # a pipe or device never holds the run
    except OSError as error:
        raise Broken(f"cannot read {what}: {error.strerror}") from None
    with os.fdopen(fd, "rb") as handle:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(handle.fileno()).st_mode):
            raise Broken(f"{what} is not a regular file")
        data = handle.read(cap + 1)
    if len(data) > cap:
        raise Broken(f"{what} is over {cap // 1024} KiB, more than ci-local will read")
    try:
        return data.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        raise Broken(f"{what} is not UTF-8 text") from None


def tracked_paths(repo):
    """git ls-files -z, read as it arrives and stopped past MAX_TRACKED_BYTES, so a longer listing is refused before
    it is all written anywhere."""
    status, output, _errors, why = run_bounded(["git", "ls-files", "-z"], repo, git_env(repo), 120, MAX_TRACKED_BYTES)
    if why and "more than" in why:
        raise Broken(f"git lists more than {MAX_TRACKED_BYTES // (1024 * 1024)} MiB of tracked paths, more than "
                     "ci-local will read")
    if why or status != 0:
        raise Broken(f"git ls-files failed{': ' + why if why else ''}")
    return output.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")


def control_within_caps(folder, label, copied):
    """Holds the control tree, the folder plus the files the manifest copies into it, to MAX_CONTROL_FILES entries and
    MAX_CONTROL_BYTES. The copied files count first, then the folder is walked lazily and the walk stops one entry
    past the limit (folders count, so a tree of empty folders is bounded too); runs before anything is copied."""
    seen = len(copied)
    size = sum(path.lstat().st_size for path in copied[:MAX_CONTROL_FILES + 1])
    if seen > MAX_CONTROL_FILES:
        raise Broken(f"control {label} holds more than {MAX_CONTROL_FILES} entries, copied files included")
    if size > MAX_CONTROL_BYTES:
        raise Broken(f"control {label} holds more than {MAX_CONTROL_BYTES // (1024 * 1024)} MiB, copied files included")
    for path in folder.rglob("*"):
        seen += 1
        if seen > MAX_CONTROL_FILES:
            raise Broken(f"control {label} holds more than {MAX_CONTROL_FILES} entries")
        size += path.lstat().st_size if not path.is_dir() or path.is_symlink() else 0
        if size > MAX_CONTROL_BYTES:
            raise Broken(f"control {label} holds more than {MAX_CONTROL_BYTES // (1024 * 1024)} MiB")


def accounted_executables(repo, entries, others, tracked):
    """Every executable file directly under tools/gates is a gate the manifest lists or a name under [others], so a
    workflow that builds a gate's path from pieces cannot bring in a gate nobody declared; and every name under
    [others] is such an executable. A gate already declared under [others] and reached by a built path is beyond
    this check; the workflow's review covers it."""
    prefix = f"{GATES.as_posix()}/"
    executables = set()
    for path in tracked:
        name = path[len(prefix):] if path.startswith(prefix) else None
        if name and "/" not in name and ((repo / path).is_symlink() or os.access(repo / path, os.X_OK)):
            executables.add(name)  # a symlink there is a way to run something, so it must be declared too
    for name in sorted(executables - set(entries) - set(others)):
        raise Broken(f"{prefix}{name} is executable, but {MANIFEST} neither lists it as a gate nor declares it under "
                     "[others]")
    for name in sorted(set(others) - executables):
        raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} declares {name} under [others], but {prefix}{name} is not an executable here")


def unaccounted_mentions(text):
    """Every mention of tools/gates the coverage reader cannot account for, as (line number, line)."""
    loops = []
    for found in LOOP.finditer(text):
        end = DONE.search(text, found.end())
        loops.append((found.start(), end.end() if end else len(text)))
    odd = []
    for mention in MENTION.finditer(text):
        at = mention.start()
        if CALLED.match(text, at) or TABLE_MENTION.match(text, at):
            continue
        if LOOP_VARIABLE.match(text, at) and any(start <= at < end for start, end in loops):
            continue
        number = text.count("\n", 0, at) + 1
        odd.append((number, text.splitlines()[number - 1].strip()))
    for piece in PIECE.finditer(text):  # gates/ reached by a built path, such as "$root/gates/x"
        if text[max(0, piece.start() - len("tools/")):piece.start()] != "tools/":
            number = text.count("\n", 0, piece.start()) + 1
            odd.append((number, text.splitlines()[number - 1].strip()))
    return sorted(set(odd))


def manifest_data(repo):
    """The parsed manifest: a [gates] table and, optionally, an [others] table and nothing else."""
    try:
        data = tomllib.loads(read_capped(repo / MANIFEST, MAX_MANIFEST, str(MANIFEST)))
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as error:
        raise Broken(f"cannot read {MANIFEST}: {error}") from None
    if not set(data) <= {"gates", "others"} or not isinstance(data.get("gates"), dict) or \
            not isinstance(data.get("others", {}), dict):
        raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} must hold one [gates] table, an optional [others] table, and nothing else")
    return data


def load_others(repo, workflows):
    """[others]: each executable under tools/gates that ci-local does not run. Its entry is either
    { runs_in = "<workflow>" }, a tracked workflow that names tools/gates/<name> on a line outside a comment, or
    { not_run = "<reason>" } for a gate with nothing to judge yet. Any other form, a name also listed as a gate,
    or a runs_in the workflow does not back up is broken, so no gate can sit here run by nothing."""
    data = manifest_data(repo)
    others = {}
    for name, entry in data.get("others", {}).items():
        shape = f"{{ runs_in = \"{WORKFLOWS.as_posix()}/<file>\" }} or {{ not_run = \"<reason>\" }}"
        if not NAME.fullmatch(name) or not isinstance(entry, dict) or len(entry) != 1 or \
                next(iter(entry)) not in OTHER_KINDS or not isinstance(next(iter(entry.values())), str):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} [others] entry {name!r} must be {shape}")
        if name in data["gates"]:
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} lists {name} both as a gate and under [others]")
        kind, value = next(iter(entry.items()))
        if kind == "not_run" and not value.strip():
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} [others] entry {name} says not_run with no reason")
        if kind == "runs_in":
            if value not in workflows:
                raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} [others] entry {name} says it runs in {value[:200]}, which is not a tracked "
                             f"workflow directly under {WORKFLOWS}")
            if value == WORKFLOW.as_posix():
                raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} [others] entry {name} names the gates workflow, whose gates ci-local runs "
                             "with their controls; list it under [gates] instead")
            if name not in called_gates(live_text(workflows[value])):
                raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} [others] entry {name} says it runs in {value}, but no line of it outside "
                             f"a comment names {GATES.as_posix()}/{name}")
        others[name] = (kind, value)
    return others


def live_text(text):
    """What a workflow line says before its first #, line by line. Only this part can back a runs_in entry, so
    nothing written after a # (a comment, or a quoted # that only looks like one) is ever taken for a real run.
    The cost is on the safe side: a step whose command has a # ahead of the gate's name is refused, never accepted;
    write the gate first or move the # to its own line."""
    return "\n".join(line.split("#", 1)[0] for line in text.split("\n"))


def tracked_workflows(repo, tracked):
    """Every tracked workflow file directly under .github/workflows, as {path: text}, each read with its cap."""
    prefix = f"{WORKFLOWS.as_posix()}/"
    paths = sorted(p for p in tracked if p.startswith(prefix) and "/" not in p[len(prefix):]
                   and p.endswith(WORKFLOW_SUFFIXES))
    if len(paths) > MAX_WORKFLOWS:
        raise Broken(f"the repository tracks {len(paths)} workflows, more than the {MAX_WORKFLOWS} ci-local will read")
    return {path: read_capped(repo / path, MAX_WORKFLOW, path) for path in paths}


def load_manifest(repo):
    data = manifest_data(repo)
    entries = {}
    for name, entry in data["gates"].items():
        if not NAME.fullmatch(name) or not isinstance(entry, dict):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} has a malformed entry {name!r}")
        extra = set(entry) - KEYS
        if extra:
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} has unknown keys: {', '.join(sorted(extra))}")
        args = entry.get("args")
        if not isinstance(args, list) or not all(isinstance(a, str) for a in args):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} needs args, a list of strings")
        for arg in args:
            for token in re.findall(r"\{[^}]*\}", arg):
                if token not in PLACEHOLDERS:
                    raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} uses {token}; only {{repo}} and {{range}} exist")
        body = entry.get("body", False)
        timeout = entry.get("timeout", DEFAULT_TIMEOUT)
        if not isinstance(body, bool):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name}: body must be true or false")
        if not isinstance(timeout, int) or isinstance(timeout, bool) or not 1 <= timeout <= 3600:
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name}: timeout must be whole seconds from 1 to 3600")
        copy = entry.get("copy", [])
        if not isinstance(copy, list) or not all(isinstance(c, str) and c for c in copy):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name}: copy must be a list of paths")
        fixture = entry.get("fixture")
        if fixture is not None and (not isinstance(fixture, str) or not NAME.fullmatch(fixture)):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name}: fixture must name a folder under {FIXTURES}")
        if name == FIXTURES.name:
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} cannot list a gate named {name}; {FIXTURES} holds the shared fixtures")
        entries[name] = {"args": args, "body": body, "timeout": timeout, "copy": copy, "fixture": fixture}
    return entries


def check_coverage(repo, entries):
    tracked = set(tracked_paths(repo).split("\0")) - {""}
    if len(tracked) > MAX_TRACKED:
        raise Broken(f"the repository has {len(tracked)} tracked paths, more than the {MAX_TRACKED} ci-local will list")
    actions = sorted(p for p in tracked if p.startswith(".github/actions/")
                     or pathlib.PurePosixPath(p).name in ("action.yml", "action.yaml"))
    if actions:
        raise Broken(f"{actions[0][:200]} is a local action, which ci-local does not read, so a gate it runs would go "
                     "uncounted")
    workflows = tracked_workflows(repo, tracked)
    if WORKFLOW.as_posix() not in workflows:
        raise Broken(f"{WORKFLOW} is not a tracked file")
    for path, text in workflows.items():
        odd = unaccounted_mentions(text)
        if odd:
            number, line = odd[0]
            raise Broken(f"{path} line {number} mentions tools/gates in a form ci-local cannot account for, so a "
                         f"gate there could escape its controls: {line[:200]}")
    for name in sorted(called_gates(workflows[WORKFLOW.as_posix()])):
        if name not in entries:
            raise Broken(f"the gates workflow runs {name}, which {MANIFEST} does not list")
    others = load_others(repo, workflows)
    for path, text in workflows.items():
        if path == WORKFLOW.as_posix():
            continue
        for name in sorted(called_gates(text)):  # comments too: a quoted # is no comment, so count every mention
            if name in entries or others.get(name) == ("runs_in", path):
                continue
            raise Broken(f"{path} runs {GATES.as_posix()}/{name}, which {MANIFEST} neither lists as a gate nor "
                         f"declares under [others] as runs_in {path}")
    accounted_executables(repo, entries, others, tracked)
    for name, entry in entries.items():
        entry["files"] = copied_files(repo, name, entry["copy"], tracked)
        entry["fixture_files"] = fixture_files(repo, name, entry["fixture"])
        gate = repo / GATES / name
        if not gate.is_file() or not os.access(gate, os.X_OK):
            raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} lists {name}, which is not an executable in {GATES}")
        entry["kinds"] = control_kinds(repo, name)
        for kind in entry["kinds"]:
            folder = repo / CONTROLS / name / kind
            if entry["body"] and not (folder / BODY).is_file():
                raise Broken(f"{name} reads a pull request body, and its {kind} control has no {BODY}")
            copied = [repo / path for path in entry["files"]]
            control_within_caps(folder, f"{name}/{kind}", [*copied, *(path for _rel, path in entry["fixture_files"])])
            if kind != "good":
                expected = folder / EXPECT
                if expected.is_symlink() or not expected.is_file() or not expected.read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip():
                    raise Broken(f"{name}/{kind} has no {EXPECT} naming the text the gate prints when it refuses")


def control_kinds(repo, name):
    """good first, then every bad and bad-<label> folder; good and bad must exist."""
    root = repo / CONTROLS / name
    for kind in ("good", "bad"):
        folder = root / kind
        if not folder.is_dir() or folder.is_symlink():
            raise Broken(f"{name} has no {kind} control")
    bad = sorted(path.name for path in root.iterdir() if path.is_dir() and BAD_FOLDER.fullmatch(path.name))
    for path in root.iterdir():
        if path.is_symlink() and BAD_FOLDER.fullmatch(path.name):
            raise Broken(f"{name}/{path.name} is a symbolic link, not a control folder")
    return ["good", *bad]


def fixture_files(repo, name, fixture):
    """The (relative path, file) pairs of the fixture a gate's controls start from: a fixed known-good tree under
    tools/ci-controls/fixtures, so a control never depends on the product it is about to judge. Regular files only,
    and no more than MAX_CONTROL_FILES of them."""
    if fixture is None:
        return []
    root = repo / FIXTURES / fixture
    if root.is_symlink() or not root.is_dir():
        raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} names fixture {fixture}, which is not a folder under {FIXTURES}")
    found = []
    for seen, path in enumerate(root.rglob("*"), 1):
        if seen > MAX_CONTROL_FILES:  # every entry counts, folders too, so the walk itself is bounded
            raise Broken(f"fixture {fixture} holds more than {MAX_CONTROL_FILES} entries")
        if path.is_symlink():
            raise Broken(f"fixture {fixture} holds a symbolic link, {path.relative_to(root)}")
        if path.is_file():
            found.append((path.relative_to(root), path))
    return sorted(found)


def copied_files(repo, name, copy, tracked):
    """The tracked regular files a copy list names: a path as given, or every tracked file under a path ending in /."""
    files = []
    for rel in copy:
        if rel.endswith("/"):
            under = sorted(path for path in tracked if path.startswith(rel))
            if not under or rel.startswith("/") or ".." in rel.split("/"):
                raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} copies {rel}, a folder holding no tracked file here")
        else:
            under = [rel]
        for path in under:
            source = repo / path
            if path not in tracked or source.is_symlink() or not source.is_file():
                raise Broken(f"{MANIFEST} entry {name} copies {path}, which is not a tracked regular file here")
            files.append(path)
    return files


SYSTEM_FOLDERS = ("/usr/bin", "/bin", "/usr/sbin", "/sbin")
NAMED_TOOLS = ("python3", "git", "cargo", "rustc", "rustup", "cargo-clippy", "clippy-driver", "rustfmt", "cargo-fmt",
               "gitleaks", "actionlint")  # the tools gates look up by name that the system folders do not hold


def caller_tool(name):
    """Where the caller's PATH finds name, or None."""
    return shutil.which(name, path=os.environ.get("PATH", ":".join(SYSTEM_FOLDERS)))


def link_folder(folder):
    """A folder of links, one for each tool in NAMED_TOOLS the caller has, to the copy the caller's PATH finds.
    These are the caller's own tools, wrappers included; nothing else from the caller's PATH reaches a gate."""
    folder.mkdir()
    for name in NAMED_TOOLS:
        found = caller_tool(name)
        if found:
            (folder / name).symlink_to(os.path.abspath(found))  # a relative PATH entry must not leave a dangling link
    return folder


def git_env(tmp):
    """The environment for ci-local's own git commands: the folder of the caller's git and the system folders, no
    git config, and nothing created."""
    git = caller_tool("git")
    path = ":".join(dict.fromkeys([*([os.path.dirname(git)] if git else []), *SYSTEM_FOLDERS]))
    return {"PATH": path, "HOME": str(tmp), "TMPDIR": str(tmp), "LANG": "en_US.UTF-8",
            "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM": "1", "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL": "/dev/null"}


def gate_env(tmp, body=None):
    """The whole environment one gate or control invocation sees. Each call makes a folder of its own under the run's
    scratch, holding a fresh HOME, CARGO_HOME and TMPDIR, so nothing one invocation leaves can reach another and no
    Cargo config on this Mac changes a result; RUSTUP_HOME alone points at the installed toolchains."""
    own = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="gate-", dir=str(tmp)))
    for name in ("home", "cargo-home", "tmp"):
        (own / name).mkdir()
    tools = link_folder(own / "bin")
    env = {"PATH": ":".join([str(tools), *SYSTEM_FOLDERS]), "HOME": str(own / "home"),
           "CARGO_HOME": str(own / "cargo-home"),
           "TMPDIR": str(own / "tmp"), "LANG": "en_US.UTF-8", "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM": "1",
           "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL": "/dev/null"}
    rustup = os.environ.get("RUSTUP_HOME") or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".rustup")
    if os.environ.get("RUSTUP_HOME") or os.path.isdir(rustup):
        env["RUSTUP_HOME"] = rustup
    if body is not None:
        env["PR_BODY"] = body
    return env


def run_gate(gate, args, cwd, env, timeout):
    """(status, stdout, stderr, why) for one gate, under run_bounded with the OUTPUT_CAP: status is None when it did
    not finish in time or wrote past the cap, and why says which. The gate's process group is stopped on every way
    out, an interrupt of ci-local included; a process that moved to a session of its own is not in that group."""
    return run_bounded([gate, *args], cwd, env, timeout, OUTPUT_CAP)


STOPS = (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP)


@contextlib.contextmanager
def stops_held():
    """Blocks the stop signals while cleanup runs, so a stop cannot cut it short; one that arrives meanwhile is
    delivered when the block ends, after the group is ended and the leader reaped."""
    held = signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, STOPS)
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_SETMASK, held)


def keep_child_status():
    """SIGCHLD back to its default, so the kernel keeps every exited child until it is collected here; a launcher
    that ignores SIGCHLD would otherwise let children vanish, their ids free for reuse and their status lost."""
    try:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_DFL)
    except ValueError:
        pass  # not the main thread; leader_held still refuses to signal a group it does not hold


def leader_held(process):
    """True while the process is still this one's child to collect, running or a zombie: only then are its pid and
    group id still its own. False once something else reaped it; that group is never signalled."""
    try:
        os.waitid(os.P_PID, process.pid, os.WEXITED | os.WNOHANG | os.WNOWAIT)
    except ChildProcessError:
        process.lost = True
        return False
    return True


def leader_was_held(process):
    """False when the leader was found already reaped by something else, its status lost."""
    return not getattr(process, "lost", False)


def leader_exited(process):
    """True once the process has exited, seen with waitid and WNOWAIT so it is not reaped: until reap() collects it,
    the zombie keeps its pid and its group id taken, so a signal sent to the group cannot reach a reused id. A
    leader something else already reaped counts as exited, and leader_held keeps its group from any signal."""
    try:
        return os.waitid(os.P_PID, process.pid, os.WEXITED | os.WNOHANG | os.WNOWAIT) is not None
    except ChildProcessError:
        process.lost = True
        return True


def wait_exit(process, seconds):
    """Waits up to seconds for the process to exit, without reaping it. True when it has exited."""
    end = time.monotonic() + max(0.0, seconds)
    while not leader_exited(process):
        if time.monotonic() >= end:
            return False
        time.sleep(0.02)
    return True


def reap(process):
    """Collects the leader once its group has been signalled for the last time; never before. Stop signals held.
    False when the leader could not be collected within five seconds; the caller makes that a broken run."""
    with stops_held():
        if getattr(process, "lost", False) is True:
            return True  # something else collected it (seen by leader_exited or leader_held); reported by the caller
        try:
            process.wait(timeout=5)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            return False
    return True


def stop_group(process):
    """Ends the gate's process group while its leader is still unreaped: SIGTERM, up to 5 seconds for the leader to
    exit, then SIGKILL for whatever is left. Run after every gate, since a gate that exits can leave children in its
    group; the caller reaps the leader afterward with reap(). The stop signals are held for the whole of it."""
    with stops_held():
        end_group(process)


def end_group(process):
    """SIGTERM, up to five seconds for the leader to exit, SIGKILL; never reaps. Called only under stops_held."""
    for number in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGKILL):
        if not leader_held(process):
            return  # its group id may already belong to someone else
        try:
            os.killpg(process.pid, number)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
            break
        if number == signal.SIGTERM:
            wait_exit(process, 5)
            time.sleep(0.05)  # let the group's other members take the signal before the next is sent
    wait_exit(process, 5)


def build_control(folder, target, env, repo, copy, fixture=()):
    """A two-commit repository from a control folder, laid over the gate's fixture, if any, and the listed tracked
    files of this repository; returns the range and the body text, if any."""
    target.mkdir(parents=True)
    git(["init", "-q", "-b", "main"], target, env)
    git(["commit", "-q", "--allow-empty", "-m", "Control base"], target, env)
    first = git(["rev-parse", "HEAD"], target, env).strip()
    for rel, path in fixture:
        place(path, rel, target)
    for rel in copy:
        (target / rel).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(repo / rel, target / rel)
    message, body = "Control fixture\n", None
    for path in sorted(folder.rglob("*")):
        rel = path.relative_to(folder)
        if path.is_symlink():
            raise Broken(f"control {folder} holds a symbolic link, {rel}")
        if path.is_dir():
            continue
        if rel.as_posix() == MESSAGE:
            message = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        elif rel.as_posix() == BODY:
            body = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        elif rel.as_posix() == EXPECT:
            continue
        else:
            place(path, rel, target)
    git(["add", "-A"], target, env)
    git(["commit", "-q", "--allow-empty", "-m", message], target, env)
    return f"{first}..HEAD", body


def place(path, rel, target):
    """Writes one control or fixture file into the control repository: a name ending in .template loses that suffix
    and has its placeholders expanded, so a Cargo.toml or source file kept for a control is never read as part of
    this repository by the gates themselves; any other file is copied as it is."""
    if rel.name.endswith(TEMPLATE) and len(rel.name) > len(TEMPLATE):
        out = target / rel.with_name(rel.name[:-len(TEMPLATE)])
        out.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        out.write_text(expand_template(path, rel), encoding="utf-8")
    else:
        (target / rel).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(path, target / rel)


def expand_template(path, rel):
    """The fixture text with {{}} removed and each {{U+XXXX}} replaced by that character; any other {{...}} is an
    error, so a typo cannot quietly leave a control that tests nothing."""
    def one(match):
        inner = match.group(1)
        if inner == "":
            return ""
        point = CODE_POINT.fullmatch(inner)
        if point and int(point.group(1), 16) <= 0x10FFFF and not 0xD800 <= int(point.group(1), 16) <= 0xDFFF:
            return chr(int(point.group(1), 16))
        raise Broken(f"control template {rel.as_posix()} has the placeholder {{{{{inner}}}}}; "
                     "only {{}} and {{U+XXXX}} exist")
    return PLACEHOLDER.sub(one, path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))


def expand(args, repo, commit_range):
    return [arg.replace("{repo}", str(repo)).replace("{range}", commit_range) for arg in args]


def run_controls(repo, entries, tmp):
    problems, count = [], 0
    for name, entry in entries.items():
        for kind in entry["kinds"]:
            wanted = 0 if kind == "good" else 1
            folder = repo / CONTROLS / name / kind
            work = tmp / "controls" / name / kind
            env = dict(git_env(tmp), **IDENTITY)
            commit_range, body = build_control(folder, work / "repo", env, repo, entry["files"], entry["fixture_files"])
            status, output, errors, why = run_gate(repo / GATES / name,
                                                   expand(entry["args"], work / "repo", commit_range), work / "repo",
                                                   gate_env(tmp, body if entry["body"] else None), entry["timeout"])
            count += 1
            said = errors or output
            if why:
                problems.append((f"control {name}/{kind} {why}", b""))
            elif TRACEBACK in output or TRACEBACK in errors:
                problems.append((f"control {name}/{kind} crashed (a Python traceback), which is never a verdict", said))
            elif status != wanted:
                problems.append((f"control {name}/{kind} exited {status}, wanted {wanted}", said))
            elif kind != "good":
                expected = (folder / EXPECT).read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
                if expected.encode("utf-8") not in output + errors:
                    problems.append((f"control {name}/{kind} refused without printing what {EXPECT} names: "
                                     f"{expected[:200]}", said))
    return problems, count


def run_tree(repo, entries, commit_range, body, tmp):
    worst, counts = 0, {"passed": 0, "failed": 0, "broken": 0, "not run": 0}
    commits = int(git(["rev-list", "--count", commit_range], repo, git_env(tmp)).strip() or 0)
    for name, entry in entries.items():
        if entry["body"] and body is None:
            say(f"NOT RUN {name} (no pull request body)")
            counts["not run"] += 1
            continue
        if commits == 0 and any("{range}" in arg for arg in entry["args"]):
            say(f"NOT RUN {name} (no commit in the range)")
            counts["not run"] += 1
            continue
        status, output, errors, why = run_gate(repo / GATES / name, expand(entry["args"], repo, commit_range), repo,
                                               gate_env(tmp, body if entry["body"] else None), entry["timeout"])
        crashed = TRACEBACK in output or TRACEBACK in errors
        if status == 0 and not crashed:
            say(f"PASS {name}")
            counts["passed"] += 1
            continue
        detail = errors.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() or output.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
        if status == 1 and not crashed:
            say(f"FAIL {name}")
            counts["failed"] += 1
            worst = max(worst, 1)
        else:
            reason = why or ("a Python traceback" if crashed else f"exit {status}")
            say(f"BROKEN {name} ({reason})")
            counts["broken"] += 1
            worst = 2
        for line in detail.splitlines()[:20]:
            say(f"    {line}")
    say(f"summary: {counts['passed']} passed, {counts['failed']} failed, {counts['broken']} broken, "
        f"{counts['not run']} not run")
    return worst


def judged_clone(caller, revision, tmp):
    """A throwaway clone sharing the caller's objects, checked out at the commit the revision names, so the gates
    read exactly that commit; nothing is written to the caller's repository."""
    env = git_env(tmp)
    status, output, _errors, why = run_bounded(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{revision}^{{commit}}"],
                                               caller, env, 60, GIT_OUTPUT_CAP)
    sha = output.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
    if why or status != 0 or not sha:
        raise Broken(f"--commit {revision} names no commit in this repository")
    target = tmp / "judged"
    git(["clone", "--quiet", "--shared", "--no-checkout", str(caller), str(target)], tmp, env, timeout=300)
    git(["checkout", "--quiet", "--detach", sha], target, env, timeout=300)
    return target


def parse(argv):
    options = {"base": "origin/main", "body": None, "controls_only": False, "commit": None}
    items = list(argv)
    while items:
        item = items.pop(0)
        if item == "--controls-only":
            options["controls_only"] = True
        elif item in ("--base", "--body", "--commit") and items:
            options[item[2:]] = items.pop(0)
        else:
            raise Broken(f"unexpected argument {item!r}; see the usage at the top of tools/ci-local")
    return options


def main(argv):
    try:
        options = parse(argv)
        status, output, _errors, why = run_bounded(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], pathlib.Path.cwd(), None,
                                                   60, GIT_OUTPUT_CAP)
        if why or status != 0:
            raise Broken("run it inside the repository")
        caller = pathlib.Path(output.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").strip())
        body = None
        if options["body"] is not None:
            body = read_capped(pathlib.Path(options["body"]), MAX_BODY, "the pull request body")
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="ci-local-") as scratch:
            tmp = pathlib.Path(scratch)
            repo = caller if options["commit"] is None else judged_clone(caller, options["commit"], tmp)
            entries = load_manifest(repo)
            check_coverage(repo, entries)
            problems, count = run_controls(repo, entries, tmp)
            if problems:
                for problem, said in problems:
                    complain(f"ci-local: refused: {problem}")
                    for line in said.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip().splitlines()[:10]:
                        complain(f"    {line[:300]}")
                complain("ci-local: refused: a gate whose controls misbehave proves nothing, so this repository was "
                         "not checked")
                return 2
            say(f"controls: {count} of {count} behaved")
            if options["controls_only"]:
                return 0
            if options["base"] == "none":  # nothing is published yet: every commit up to the judged one is new
                commit_range = git(["rev-parse", "--verify", "HEAD^{commit}"], repo, git_env(tmp)).strip()
            else:
                base = git(["rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{options['base']}^{{commit}}"], caller,
                           git_env(tmp)).strip()
                commit_range = f"{base}..HEAD"
            return run_tree(repo, entries, commit_range, body, tmp)
    except Broken as error:
        complain(f"ci-local: broken: {error}")
        return 2
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        complain("ci-local: broken: interrupted; every gate it started was stopped, and nothing was judged")
        return 2
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        complain(f"ci-local: broken: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
        return 2


STOPPING = []


def interrupted(_number, _frame):
    """The first stop raises KeyboardInterrupt, which runs the cleanup; later stops change nothing."""
    if STOPPING:
        return
    STOPPING.append(True)
    raise KeyboardInterrupt


if __name__ == "__main__":
    keep_child_status()
    for number in STOPS:
        signal.signal(number, interrupted)  # Ctrl-C, a stop and a hang-up all run the same cleanup, once
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
